{"albumId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef","candidateContentHash":"69ad5bae4423dae72851b66c2708f19532d84e0be50b0c55cb869e9e1c3360bc","decisions":[{"artifactIds":["673f59fdb06ee0700a587b9d2a6b2a28bf421f014cace3ba8ca5a9f54343cb11"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:1:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:1:fact-1","rationale":"The retained artifact text at the cited offsets exactly matches the extract and states that the song was the final song recorded for the album and was requested by Tom Wilson as another potential single with Nico lead vocals.","sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sunday_Morning_(The_Velvet_Underground_song)"]},{"artifactIds":["fde6fa321d2d085c5f7c3da51cf0f683232ad83b68e85302595cfc5e1bc3aa35"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:2:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:2:fact-1","rationale":"The cited offset slice exactly matches the extract, which directly says the lyrics describe a man's efforts to obtain heroin.","sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I%27m_Waiting_for_the_Man"]},{"artifactIds":["b972083b6b74fcd96e0b2c56e39d6c9bac2c1e0fc7af18a7e70951edb5a7d5e4"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:3:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:3:fact-1","rationale":"The cited offset slice exactly matches the extract, which states that Lou Reed wrote the song about Warhol superstar Edie Sedgwick at Andy Warhol's request.","sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Femme_Fatale_(The_Velvet_Underground_song)"]},{"artifactIds":["2b5a599e33f528b318e2a8cd461b0dd02fa54474d47994eb1090034807ad352a"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:4:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:4:fact-1","rationale":"The retained text slice exactly matches the extract and directly states that the song was inspired by Sacher-Masoch's novel and includes sadomasochism and bondage themes.","sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Venus_in_Furs_(song)"]},{"artifactIds":["b93ae3a87cefc50d6f300ef1fba9c1a26629b77ac265367703c0fced79dcb25a"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:5:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:5:fact-1","rationale":"The cited retained-text slice exactly matches the extract, which says Lou Reed wrote the song on the back of an envelope while the band was traveling to a Cafe Bizarre gig.","sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Run_Run_Run_(The_Velvet_Underground_song)"]},{"artifactIds":["3ee567511a4c14ca1f2416f65a1808a3ebc3bb64ebcc40b5f8764cc2e0bc3b76"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:6:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:6:fact-1","rationale":"The cited offset slice exactly matches the extract and states that the inspiration came from Reed's observation of Warhol's circle, including the quoted Reed description of Factory people.","sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/All_Tomorrow%27s_Parties"]},{"artifactIds":["13214a85df1080c980e86aff560a20ed872080f7c9730fbbde9da53f48b45b7a"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:7:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:7:fact-1","rationale":"The retained artifact slice exactly matches the extract and directly states that Lou Reed wrote the song in 1964 and that it overtly depicts heroin usage and its effects.","sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heroin_(The_Velvet_Underground_song)"]},{"artifactIds":["f32e2d0743d53ba8ccd0151f7f5aa43f53fcf171ccf654a193e8128a51ae85e1"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:8:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:8:fact-1","rationale":"The cited offset slice exactly matches the extract and says the syncopated guitar riff was taken from Marvin Gaye's 1962 song Hitch Hike.","sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/There_She_Goes_Again"]},{"artifactIds":["5fad567c46eec2ed3361593a982ab549dab2de8a3ef4b54510eca5ff712054e3"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:9:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:9:fact-1","rationale":"The cited retained-text slice exactly matches the extract, which directly states that Lou Reed wrote the song for Nico and that Nico provides lead vocals.","sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I%27ll_Be_Your_Mirror"]},{"artifactIds":["0b53d014d4728cc4c49b5aaa0009059d67e26ae9b58459227cf38b1c7bd71833"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:10:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:10:fact-1","rationale":"The retained artifact slice exactly matches the extract and directly states that John Cale's electric viola dominates the music and creates dissonance throughout the song.","sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Black_Angel%27s_Death_Song"]},{"artifactIds":["fd8ec723d424b13c74d3533e135bf98f51331c554f4fa355c774736c86893902"],"claimId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1:11:fact-1","decision":"supported","decisionId":"023-the-velvet-underground-the-velvet-underground-and-nico-0913adef:1: